                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It doesn't make much sense what you are meant to do when you first boot up, do not expect a tutorial. But I gathered you collect crystals and go higher in 'Asend mode' and leap was self explanatory.\n\nDepth perception and perspective are things this game does not do well. \nDangerous obstacles like spinning blades and lasers are extremely difficult to know how close they are to your hitbox and the player feels unreasonable punished when hit. But there doesn't seem to be any set of rules set to understand how to handle them. (especially if its random rotation and position) a few times the blade is right over the crystal. (how am I meant to get that?) so depth perception makes it difficult to judge distances. \n\nPerspective is also something else that makes it difficult to judge distances and how your shift boost works. Due to the angle you are ascending and the stairs been sharp right angles. alot of obstacles are not seen until the very last second. Most of this comes down to the first person perspective. The camera postion makes it difficult in many situations. \n\nOverall for the price its pretty cool. But its the kind of game you play once and not really after that. This is really because the two major things I mentioned above  Depth perception and perspective.  Other than that I can see someone getting enjoyment out of it. \n\nedit 1: booted it up again, 8 years later, nothing changed. game is still very much unplayable. its missing a lot of visual and audio indicators to give the player some kind of heads up. I discovered some more things you face. randomly balls that follow you around but you don't know where or when they are close. played for 15 minutes again, and Uninstalled. not worth my time. changing the review to not recommended.",
